course: world
lesson: 49
title: Map reading puzzles
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: To read a map well you combine three things: the directions, the scale and the legend.
explain: Directions turn with you: if you face north, east is on your right and west on your left; if you turn around to face south, east is now on your left.
explain: The scale turns map centimetres into real distances: on a map where 1 centimetre is 2 kilometres, a path of 5 centimetres is 10 kilometres; walking about 4 kilometres an hour, that takes two and a half hours.
explain: The Sun helps when there is no compass: it rises roughly in the east and sets roughly in the west; in the United States, Canada, Britain and Ireland it stands in the south at midday, so at noon your shadow points north, but in Australia and New Zealand the midday Sun is in the north and the shadow points south.
explain: Moss on one side of a tree is not a safe guide, because moss grows wherever the bark stays shady and damp; the Sun, the stars and a compass are far more reliable.
fact: If you face north, east is on your right, west on your left and south behind you.
fact: If you face south, east is on your left and west on your right.
fact: To find a real distance, multiply the map distance in centimetres by the distance one centimetre stands for.
fact: The Sun rises roughly in the east and sets roughly in the west.
fact: In the United States, Canada, Britain and Ireland the Sun is in the south at midday, so shadows at noon point north.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand the Sun is in the north at midday, so shadows at noon point south.
fact: The North Star, Polaris, shows the direction of north at night in the Northern Hemisphere.
fact: Moss does not grow only on the north side of trees, so it is not a reliable guide.
fact: Rivers always flow from higher land to lower land, so a river flows away from the mountains.
fact: A larger scale, such as 1 centimetre to 10 metres, shows a small area in great detail.
fact: Turning a quarter of a full turn to the right changes north to east, east to south, south to west and west to north.
exercise: You face north. Which direction is on your left? | West | East | South | North-east
exercise: You face south. Which direction is on your right? | West | East | North | South-east
exercise: You face east and turn a quarter turn to the right. Which way do you face now? | South | North | West | North-east
exercise: You face west and turn right around to face the other way. Which way do you face now? | East | North | South | West
exercise: On a map 1 centimetre stands for 5 kilometres. A road on the map is 6 centimetres. How long is the real road? | 30 kilometres | 11 kilometres | 6 kilometres | 56 kilometres
exercise: On a map 1 centimetre stands for 100 metres. The school is 2,000 metres from the park. How far apart are they on the map? | 20 centimetres | 2 centimetres | 200 centimetres | 100 centimetres
exercise: At noon in Chicago, in the USA, which way does your shadow point? | North | South | East | West
exercise: You watch the sunrise from your window. Which direction does the window face, roughly? | East | West | North | South
exercise: Which star helps to find north at night? | The North Star, Polaris | The Sun | The brightest planet | Any star that twinkles
exercise: A lost hiker sees moss on one side of a tree. What should she know? | Moss grows on any damp shady side, so it is not a safe guide | Moss always grows on the north side of trees | Moss always grows on the south side of trees | Moss always points towards the nearest village
exercise: A river flows from the mountains in the north. Which way is it most likely flowing? | Southwards, away from the mountains | Northwards, up into the mountains | In a circle around the mountains | Both ways at once
exercise: The map scale is 1 centimetre = 1 kilometre. You walk 4 kilometres an hour. How long will an 8-centimetre path take? | 2 hours | 8 hours | 4 hours | 32 hours
exercise: Which map is best for finding your way around the rooms of a museum? | A plan with a large scale | A map of the whole world | A globe of the Earth | A map of all of Europe
exercise: You face north-east and turn to face exactly the opposite way. Which way do you face now? | South-west | South-east | North-west | South
exercise: On a map Lake A is directly above Village B. In which direction is the lake from the village? | North | South | East | West
test: Sam walks north out of his door, turns right at the corner and then turns right again. Which way is he walking now? | South | North | East | West
test: Two towns are 12 centimetres apart on a map where 1 centimetre is 10 kilometres. A bus drives 60 kilometres an hour. How long is the trip in a straight line? | 2 hours | 12 hours | 1 hour | 6 hours
test: In the evening you see the Sun going down straight ahead of you. Which way are you looking, roughly? | West | East | North | South
test: A boy in Melbourne stands with his back to the midday Sun. Which way is he facing? | South | North | East | West
test: On a map the school is 3 centimetres east of the house and the shop 3 centimetres west of the house. The scale is 1 centimetre = 100 metres. How far is the school from the shop? | 600 metres | 300 metres | 100 metres | 0 metres
test: Two maps show the same park. On the first, 1 centimetre is 10 metres; on the second, 1 centimetre is 1 kilometre. Which shows the benches and paths in detail? | The first map | The second map | Both the same | Neither of them
test: A boat floats down a river without rowing, and the river runs from the hills to the sea. Where will the boat drift? | Towards the sea | Towards the hills | Nowhere, it stays still | First to the sea, then back to the hills
test: At night in the forest you see the North Star straight in front of you. Which direction is on your right? | East | West | South | North
test: On a map the forest is 4 centimetres south of the river and the scale is 1 centimetre = 500 metres. What does a walker crossing from the forest to the river do? | Walks 2 kilometres north | Walks 2 kilometres south | Walks 4 kilometres north | Walks 500 metres north
test: facing north, turn right, which way am i facing now | East | West | South | North
test: midday in melbourne, which way does my shadow point | South | North | East | West
test: my scout leader said moss always grows on the north side of trees. true? | No, moss grows on any side, so it is no good guide | Yes, that is the best way to find north | Yes, but only in winter | No, moss always grows on the south side
test: Ella says rivers flow towards the mountains. right? | No, rivers flow from higher land to lower land, away from the mountains | Yes, rivers flow up into the mountains | Yes, the water is pulled by the peaks | No, rivers do not flow at all
exam: It is given that a person faces south. Ascertain the direction lying to that person's left. | East | West | North | South
exam: It is given that one centimetre upon a map represents two kilometres. Determine the real distance represented by five centimetres. | Ten kilometres | Seven kilometres | Two and a half kilometres | Five kilometres
exam: Identify the direction in which shadows point at noon in Australia. | South | North | East | West
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning moss upon trees. | It is not a reliable guide to north | It grows only upon the north side | It grows only upon the south side | It grows only where sunlight falls
